---
description: Persisted browser data must keep loading after schema changes and migrations.
alwaysApply: true
---

# Settings compatibility

Users already have a data folder (`%APPDATA%\com.ce.browser`). It holds `settings.json`, `session.json`, `bookmarks.json`, `downloads.json` and `extensions.json`. The WebView2 profile lives in `%LOCALAPPDATA%\com.ce.browser\profile`. A new build must open those files, keep every value the user already set, and apply defaults only for data the file does not contain.

- Add a field with `#[serde(default)]` on the struct, plus a `Default` value. An older file that omits the key must still deserialize. Mirror the field in `types/browser.ts` and in the UI that reads it. Wire names stay `camelCase`.
- Rename or move a field by still reading the old key (`#[serde(alias = "...")]` or an explicit load) and writing the new key. Do not make the user edit the file.
- Change what a value means by mapping the old value on load. Retired or unknown values fall back to the current default (`BrowserSettings::normalize`). A bad value is not a reason to reject the file.
- Removing a field must leave old files loadable. Do not add `deny_unknown_fields`.
- Run a migration on read. The next save writes the current shape. Loading an already-current file must change nothing.
- A file that fails to parse is copied to `<name>.json.bak` by `files::read_json` before defaults are used. Keep that behaviour for new stores.

```rust
#[derive(Deserialize, Serialize)]
#[serde(default, rename_all = "camelCase")]
pub struct BrowserSettings {
    #[serde(alias = "searchProvider")]
    pub search_engine: String,
}
```
